    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i think i see. so it's basically a correlation test. but what happens if the signal is extremely noisy or has random spikes? does the transform still work or does the noise just mess everything up? like how do we distinguish between the actual intended signal and just random fluctuations?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Fourier transform treats noise mathematically just like any other frequency, so random spikes will spread their energy across the frequency spectrum rather than being easily identified as a single \"wrong\" note. Distinguishing a true signal from random noise depends largely on whether specific frequencies have sharp, high-purity peaks or if they are dull, broad bumps resulting from interference; however, for very harsh random noise, engineers often use additional techniques like averaging or filtering to denoise the data before or after the analysis. Essentially, while the transform doesn't magically separate them, it provides the raw data allowing you to see if the \"signal\" has a unique signature compared to the background static.",
